      learn: `"The Space Age began on October 4, 1957 when the Soviet Union launched
Sputnik — the first artificial satellite. The Space Race between the
US and USSR drove extraordinary advances in just 12 years.

Key milestones:

1961 — Yuri Gagarin becomes the first human in space (Soviet Union)

1969 — Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin walk on the Moon (USA, Apollo
11)

1971 — First space station (Salyut 1, Soviet Union)

1981-2011 — NASA Space Shuttle program (135 missions)

1998-present — International Space Station (ISS), a continuous human
presence in space for 25+ years

Modern space exploration:

NASA's Artemis program aims to return humans to the Moon and eventually
Mars. SpaceX — founded by Elon Musk — has revolutionized rocketry
with reusable rockets and is developing Starship, the most powerful
rocket ever built.

Blue Origin, Virgin Galactic, and hundreds of private companies are
making space more accessible than ever.

Robotics in space:

Mars rovers — Curiosity and Perseverance — have been exploring Mars,
analyzing rocks, searching for signs of past life. Perseverance even
carried a small helicopter (Ingenuity) that made the first powered
flight on another planet.

James Webb Space Telescope — launched 2021 — looks back 13.6 billion
years to the first galaxies, showing us the universe as it was just
after the Big Bang.

The future:

Mars colonization — humans could set foot on Mars within your
lifetime. One-way missions and eventually permanent settlements.

Space mining — asteroids contain enormous quantities of rare metals.
One asteroid could contain more platinum than has ever been mined on
Earth.

Megaconstellations — thousands of satellites providing global internet
coverage.

Space tourism — becoming commercially available.

Why does space matter? Resources, survival of the species, scientific
knowledge, and technology spinoffs that improve life on Earth (GPS,
memory foam, water filters, scratch-resistant lenses, and CAT scans all
came from space research)."`,
